/**
   @page repro_main repro: SIP Proxy, Registrar and Presence Server

   repro is a SIP proxy server built on the @ref resip_main "SIP stack" and the
   @ref dum_main "Dialog Usage Manager". It is configured with repro.config
   and through its web administration pages. Most of it is in the reprolib
   library, so applications can embed and extend it.

   @section repro_structure Structure
      - repro::ReproRunner : Reads the configuration, then creates and runs
      everything else: the SIP stack, the datastore, the proxy, the DUM-based
      registrar and presence server, and the administration interfaces.
      Applications can subclass it and override its create...() and
      make...ProcessorChain() functions.
      - repro::Proxy : The proxy core, a resip::TransactionUser running in its
      own thread. Each request it receives gets a repro::RequestContext,
      which holds the request, its targets and their responses
      (repro::ResponseContext) until the transaction completes.
      - repro::Registrar : Handles REGISTER requests, through DUM's
      resip::ServerRegistration, and stores bindings in DUM's
      registration database.
      - repro::PresenceServer : Handles PUBLISH and SUBSCRIBE for presence.

   @section repro_chains Processor Chains
      A request is handled by three chains of repro::Processor objects, each
      returning a repro::Processor::processor_action_t (Continue,
      WaitingForEvent, SkipThisChain or SkipAllChains):
      - The request chain (the "monkeys", in repro/monkeys) decides where a
      request goes. As built by ReproRunner, it fixes strict routes
      (StrictRouteFixup), recognizes trusted peers (IsTrustedNode),
      authenticates the sender (CertificateAuthenticator for TLS client
      certificates; DigestAuthenticator, or RADIUSAuthenticator when a
      RADIUS server is configured; or CookieAuthenticator for WebSocket
      clients), checks that it is responsible for the request
      (AmIResponsible), applies request filters (RequestFilter), applies
      static routes (StaticRoute, or SimpleStaticRoute when the Routes
      setting is used), looks up registered contacts (LocationServer), and,
      if enabled, stores MESSAGE requests for offline users (MessageSilo).
      - The response chain (the "lemurs") handles responses: moving on to the
      next registered flow when an Outbound (RFC 5626) flow has failed
      (OutboundTargetHandler) and, if enabled, following 3xx redirects
      (RecursiveRedirect).
      - The target chain (the "baboons") decides the order in which targets
      are tried: optionally by distance (GeoProximityTargetSorter), by q-value
      (QValueTargetHandler), and finally SimpleTargetHandler.

   @section repro_data Datastore
      repro::Store gives access to users, routes, ACLs, request filters,
      static registrations, stored messages, TLS peer identities and
      settings, kept through a repro::AbstractDb: repro::BerkeleyDb by
      default, or repro::MySqlDb or repro::PostgreSqlDb.

   @section repro_admin Administration and Extensions
      - repro::WebAdmin : The HTML administration pages, served by
      repro's own HTTP server. repro::RestAdmin serves a JSON REST API under
      /api/v1/ on the same listener.
      - repro::CommandServer : A simple XML command interface over TCP, used by
      the reprocmd tool.
      - repro::RegSyncServer and repro::RegSyncClient : Replicate
      registrations between two repro servers.
      - repro::AccountingCollector : Writes session and registration
      accounting events to persistent queues for other processes to read.
      - repro::Plugin : Loadable plugins can add processors to any of the
      chains (see repro/plugins/example, and repro/plugins/pyroute for
      routing in Python).
*/
